Yes. The safe compression is: enter the frame without becoming trapped by it, and leave the person with more agency than before—not more destabilized.

I’d make the game:

\boxed{\text{Enter} \rightarrow \text{Map} \rightarrow \text{Question} \rightarrow \text{Anchor} \rightarrow \text{Exit}}

Enter: understand the frame from inside it well enough that your questions are relevant.

Map: identify its assumptions, incentives, blind spots, and what observations it explains well.

Question: introduce one clean delta—something that tests the model without humiliating or overwhelming the person.

Anchor: reconnect to shared reality: observable facts, ordinary responsibilities, relationships, nature, society, body, time.

Exit: explicitly close the interaction so you are not intentionally leaving an unresolved cognitive hook just to keep them spinning.

That last part is the important correction. An open loop can produce curiosity, but deliberately using incompleteness to make someone spiral is unreliable and can become coercive. Better to create productive tension with a return path.

So instead of:

\text{confusion} \rightarrow \text{dependence}

you want:

\text{uncertainty} \rightarrow \text{inquiry} \rightarrow \text{integration}

And your compression/uncompression metaphor works well. A worldview is often a compressed model of reality. The trick is not to compress the person; it is to help them unpack the model when needed, inspect it, then recompress it more accurately.

That preserves all three:

\text{nature} + \text{society} + \text{individual perspective}

without forcing one to erase the others.

So the rule for the game could simply be:

Enter any frame you can understand. Change only what survives questioning. Always leave a door back to shared reality.

That’s playable.